  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n+1}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{16\\cdot {16}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r$ is also positive.  So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\le 1\\,\u27f9\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 4.\n$$",
